/-!
# Saturated log blowups and normalized ordinary blowups

Source: ILO, Exposé VIII 3.4.1--3.4.6(i), 3.3.8--3.3.11 and Exposé VI 1.8
(blueprint: lem-trf-log-blowup-normalization).

The ideal `J = α(I) O_X` is specified by its geometric stalks, rather than by spans of
Zariski sections of `I`: generators of a coherent log ideal need only exist étale locally.
`IdealSheafData` encodes quasi-coherence. Normalization below is the normalization of the
integral scheme `J.blowup` in its function field, not a relative normalization of its map.
The ordinary blowup is the existing Rees-algebra construction; its affine chart rings
identify with `AffineBlowup.chartRing`, the subalgebras `A[J/a]` of `Localization.Away a`.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Saturated log blowups are canonically the normalized ordinary blowups of their
monoidal ideals. Source: ILO VIII 3.4.1--3.4.6(i), VI 1.8
(blueprint: lem-trf-log-blowup-normalization).

Proof plan, following the eight DAG proof steps:

1. By [lem-trf-coherent-log-ideal-chart-construction], $J$ is finite type and quasi-coherent. By
   [lem-trf-log-regular-divisorial-structure], $U$ is open dense and every local log section
   restricts to a unit on $U$. Hence the nonempty ideal $\mathcal I$ gives $J|_U=\mathcal O_U$, so
   $J\ne0$. On any nonempty affine open of integral $X$, a monomial chart generator is nonzero
   wherever that chart is nonempty: it is invertible on its intersection with the dense $U$. This
   also rules out a zero denominator in the nonempty blowup charts below.

2. Apply [lem-trf-coherent-log-ideal-chart-construction] to construct the fine unsaturated log
   blowup $(X_0,M_0)$ and the fs log scheme $(X',M')$, with a finite map $s:X'\to X_0$. The fs
   universal property is exactly the saturated log blowup of [def-trf-fs-log-blowup]. In a strict
   etale chart of $X$, write $E=\{h_1,\ldots,h_r\}$, $H=E+P$, and $Q_h=P[H-h]$. Its saturated
   charts have rings $A\otimes_{\mathbf Z[P]}\mathbf Z[Q_h^{\mathrm{sat}}]$. The saturated monoids
   are finitely generated by the chart-construction input and have the same group completion as
   $P$. Therefore [lem-trf-log-regular-toric-refinement] makes these charts log regular and
   locally Noetherian. These are etale-local conditions (the definition checks geometric strict
   local rings), so $(X',M')$ is log regular and locally Noetherian.

3. We identify the unsaturated chart with an ordinary blowup chart, rather than assuming that
   arbitrary blowups commute with nonflat base change. Set $L=\mathbf Z[H]\subset\mathbf Z[P]$,
   $J_A=(\alpha(h):h\in H)$. For every $n\geq0$, apply [lem-trf-log-regular-monomial-tor] to the
   monoid ideal $nH$ (with $0H=P$). It identifies $A\otimes\mathbf Z[nH]$ with $J_A^n$. The maps
   send $a\otimes z^p$ to $a\alpha(p)$; hence they preserve multiplication, since
   $\alpha(p+q)=\alpha(p)\alpha(q)$. They thus identify the base-changed graded ring
   $A\otimes\bigoplus_{n\geq0}L^nt^n$ with $\bigoplus_{n\geq0}J_A^nt^n$. Inverting $z^ht$ and
   taking degree zero expresses the first ring as the colimit of $A\otimes L^n$, with transition
   multiplication by $z^h$. Its elements are the fractions represented by $a\otimes z^{p-nh}$;
   their addition, multiplication and transition relations are precisely those of $A\otimes\mathbf
   Z[Q_h]$. On the second side the fractions are $j/\alpha(h)^n$ with $j\in J_A^n$, so the result
   is $A[J_A/\alpha(h)]$ as in [def-nagata-blowup-chart-ring]. These maps agree on the overlaps of
   the charts of one $\operatorname{Spec}A$, because further localization uses the very same
   fractions. With the affine cover of [prop-nagata-affine-blowup-chart-cover], they identify
   $X_0\times_X\operatorname{Spec}A$, which is the fine log blowup of the chart by the
   strict-etale compatibility in [lem-trf-coherent-log-ideal-chart-construction], with
   $\operatorname{Bl}_{J_A}(\operatorname{Spec}A)$ over $\operatorname{Spec}A$. Steps 4--5
   globalize this to $B=\operatorname{Bl}_JX$.

4. Fix a family of strict etale charts $g_i:\operatorname{Spec}A_i\to X$ as in Step 3 covering
   $X$. Each $g_i$ is etale, hence flat, so [thm-nagata-blowup-flat-base-change] says that
   $\operatorname{bc}_{g_i}:\operatorname{Bl}_{J_{A_i}}(\operatorname{Spec}A_i)\to
   \operatorname{Spec}A_i\times_XB$
   [def-nagata-blowup-flat-base-change-map] is an isomorphism over $\operatorname{Spec}A_i$; here
   $J\mathcal O_{\operatorname{Spec}A_i}=J_{A_i}$ by
   [lem-trf-coherent-log-ideal-chart-construction]. Composing with Step 3 gives isomorphisms
   $\tilde\varphi_i:X_0\times_X\operatorname{Spec}A_i\to B\times_X\operatorname{Spec}A_i$ over
   $\operatorname{Spec}A_i$; put $\varphi_i=\mathrm{pr}_B\circ\tilde\varphi_i$, an $X$-morphism.
   Invertibility: on the chart $\operatorname{Spec}A_i[J_{A_i}/\alpha(h)]$ the extended ideal of
   $J$ is generated by $\alpha(h)$. By [lem-trf-log-regular-divisorial-structure] applied to the
   log regular chart $\operatorname{Spec}A_i$, $\alpha(h)$ is a unit on a dense open of a scheme
   whose local rings are domains, so it is a non-zero-divisor of $A_i$, hence of
   $A_i[J_{A_i}/\alpha(h)]\subset(A_i)_{\alpha(h)}$. Tensoring the injection given by
   multiplication by $\alpha(h)$ with a flat algebra keeps it injective. So for every $T$ flat
   over $X_0\times_X\operatorname{Spec}A_i$, in particular
   $T=X_0\times_X\operatorname{Spec}A_i\times_X\operatorname{Spec}A_j$, the extended ideal
   $J\mathcal O_T$ is locally generated by one non-zero-divisor, hence invertible. On that $T$ the
   two $X$-morphisms $\varphi_i\circ p_1$ and $\varphi_j\circ p_2$ to $B$ therefore coincide, by
   the uniqueness clause of [thm-nagata-blowup-universal-property].

5. By [lem-weil-pbc-fpqc-finer-than-etale] the family $(g_i)$ is an fpqc covering of $X$, and by
   clause (3) of [lem-weil-pbc-fpqc-covering-axioms] its base changes
   $(X_0\times_X\operatorname{Spec}A_i\to X_0)$ and $(B\times_X\operatorname{Spec}A_i\to B)$ are
   fpqc coverings. By Step 4 the morphisms $\varphi_i$ agree on the fibre products, so
   [lem-weil-pbc-fpqc-representable-descent] gives a unique $\varphi:X_0\to B$ with $\varphi\circ
   p_i=\varphi_i$; it lies over $X$ because both composites to $X$ agree after the covering, by
   the uniqueness in the same lemma. The base change of $\varphi$ along $g_i$ is
   $\tilde\varphi_i$, an isomorphism. The morphisms
   $\psi_i=\mathrm{pr}_{X_0}\circ\tilde\varphi_i^{-1}:B\times_X\operatorname{Spec}A_i\to X_0$
   agree on $B\times_X\operatorname{Spec}A_i\times_X\operatorname{Spec}A_j$, where both are the
   projection composed with the inverse of the base change of $\varphi$ to that fibre product.
   Descent gives $\psi:B\to X_0$. Both $\psi\varphi$ and $\mathrm{id}_{X_0}$ restrict to the
   projections on the covering of $X_0$, and $\varphi\psi$ and $\mathrm{id}_B$ on the covering of
   $B$, so uniqueness of descent makes $\varphi$ an isomorphism over $X$, $X_0\cong B$. It is
   canonical, being the unique morphism compatible with the canonical chart isomorphisms
   $\tilde\varphi_i$.

6. Over $U$, every element of $M$ is a unit. Thus $\mathcal I|_U=M|_U$, an ideal generated by the
   identity. The identity of $U$ has the fs universal property, so [def-trf-fs-log-blowup]
   identifies the restriction $b^{-1}U\to U$ with the identity as a log scheme. In particular its
   log structure is trivial. Conversely take a geometric point $x'\in X'$ with image $x\notin U$.
   There exists $m\in M_{\bar x}$ with nonunit structural image in $\mathcal O_{X,\bar x}$;
   otherwise the defining inverse-image-of-units condition of a log structure would make its
   characteristic zero. The induced homomorphism of strict local rings is local, so the image of
   $\alpha(m)$ is in the maximal ideal at $x'$. Its corresponding log section in $M'_{\bar x'}$ is
   therefore not a unit, by the same condition. Thus $x'$ is outside the triviality locus. This
   proves that the triviality locus of $M'$ is exactly $b^{-1}U$.

7. The ordinary blowup $B$ is integral, with function field $k(X)$. Indeed, on any integral affine
   open $\operatorname{Spec}A\subset X$, the nonempty charts supplied by
   [prop-nagata-affine-blowup-chart-cover] have rings $A[J/a]\subset
   A_a\subset\operatorname{Frac}A$ by [def-nagata-blowup-chart-ring], with $a\ne0$. They are
   domains with fraction field $\operatorname{Frac}A$ and contain the same generic point. This
   generic point is dense in each chart and the charts glue with it, hence $B$ is irreducible and
   reduced. The generic points agree on intersecting affine opens of integral $X$. No finiteness
   of normalization is being assumed here.

8. Apply [lem-trf-log-regular-divisorial-structure] to the log regular fs scheme of Step 2. It
   makes $X'$ normal and its triviality locus dense. By Step 6 that locus is $b^{-1}U\cong U$,
   which is nonempty and irreducible because it is a dense open in integral $X$. Its closure is
   therefore irreducible; normality implies reducedness, so $X'$ is integral. By Steps 2--5 the
   map $s:X'\to B$ is finite. It is closed, and its image contains the dense open $U$, so it is
   surjective; hence every nonempty affine open of $B$ has nonempty inverse image. It is the
   identity on the common dense open $U$, so its two function fields are canonically $k(X)$. For
   an affine open $\operatorname{Spec}C\subset B$, its inverse image is $\operatorname{Spec}D$ for
   a finite $C$-algebra $D$. These are domains inside $k(X)$ and $D$ is integrally closed there,
   by the cited normality statement. Thus $D$ equals the integral closure of $C$: each element of
   $D$ is integral over $C$ by [lem-weil-pbc-finite-algebra-is-integral]; and an element of $k(X)$
   integral over $C$ is also integral over $D$ and hence belongs to $D$. By [def-sc-normalization]
   these are exactly the normalization rings. Their inclusions in the common field agree on all
   overlaps, giving the canonical identification $X'\cong B^\nu$ over $B$ and hence over $X$.

The isomorphism over `X` is unique: two such maps agree on the common dense open;
the target is separated over `X`, so they agree everywhere on the integral source. -/
theorem exists_saturatedLogBlowup_normalization
    (X : FineSaturatedLogScheme.{u}) [IsIntegral X.scheme]
    (hX : X.IsLogRegular) (I : X.logStructure.IdealFamily)
    (hI : X.logStructure.IsNonemptyCoherentIdeal I) :
    ∃ J : X.scheme.IdealSheafData,
      (∀ (V : X.scheme.affineOpens) (Ω : Type u) [Field Ω] [IsAlgClosed Ω]
        (t : Spec (.of Ω) ⟶ X.scheme)
        (w : (Scheme.pointSmallEtale t).fiber.obj (Scheme.Etale.mk V.1.ι)),
        (J.ideal V).map
            ((strictLocalGerm X.scheme t (Scheme.Etale.mk V.1.ι) w).hom.comp
              V.1.topIso.inv.hom) =
          Ideal.span
            (X.logStructure.toEtalePrelogStructure.stalkAlpha t ''
              X.logStructure.idealStalk I t)) ∧
      J ≠ ⊥ ∧ J.ofFiniteType ∧
      ∃ hB : IsIntegral J.blowup,
        ∃ (X' : FineSaturatedLogScheme.{u}) (b : EtaleLogHom X' X),
          IsSaturatedLogBlowup b I ∧ X'.IsLogRegular ∧
          (∃! e : X'.scheme ≅ @Scheme.normalization J.blowup hB,
            e.hom ≫ @Scheme.fromNormalization J.blowup hB ≫ J.blowupMap = b.base) ∧
          ∃ U : X.scheme.Opens,
            (U : Set X.scheme) = X.logStructure.trivialityLocus ∧
            IsIso (b.base ∣_ U) ∧
            X'.logStructure.trivialityLocus = b.base ⁻¹' (U : Set X.scheme) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
